---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Lightbox from "../components/Lightbox.astro";
import frontispiece from "../assets/frontispiece.jpg";

const alt =
  "The frontispiece of Leviathan, 1651: a crowned giant composed of hundreds of small human figures rises above a landscape, holding a sword in one hand and a bishop's crozier in the other. Below, two columns of small panels show the instruments of civil and ecclesiastical power.";
---

<Layout title="Home" wide>
  <section class="grid items-center gap-10 md:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.15fr)_minmax(0,1fr)] md:gap-14">
    <div>
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.2em] text-stone-500 uppercase dark:text-stone-400">
        Thomas Hobbes · 1651
      </p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 font-serif text-6xl leading-none font-semibold tracking-tight md:text-7xl">Leviathan</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 font-serif text-xl leading-snug text-stone-600 italic dark:text-stone-400">
        or The Matter, Forme and Power of a Common-Wealth Ecclesiasticall and Civil
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6 text-sm tracking-wide text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">
        <span class="font-serif">Non est potestas Super Terram quae Comparetur ei.</span>
        <span class="ml-2 text-xs uppercase">Job 41</span>
      </p>
      <p class="mt-8 max-w-prose text-base leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        A reading edition of the 1651 text, with the terms Hobbes defines explained where they occur, his
        sources traced, and the book's great themes followed across its chapters.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a
          href="/chapters/01-of-sense/"
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded bg-stone-900 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-stone-50 transition-colors hover:bg-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-100 dark:text-stone-900 dark:hover:bg-stone-300"
        >
          <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <path d="M10 5.5C8.6 4.3 6.6 3.8 3 4v11c3.6-.2 5.6.3 7 1.5 1.4-1.2 3.4-1.7 7-1.5V4c-3.6-.2-5.6.3-7 1.5Z" />
            <path d="M10 5.5v11" />
          </svg>
          Start reading
        </a>
        <a
          href="/themes/the-state-of-nature/"
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded border border-stone-300 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-stone-700 transition-colors hover:border-stone-500 hover:text-stone-900 dark:border-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 dark:hover:border-stone-500 dark:hover:text-stone-100"
        >
          <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <circle cx="10" cy="10" r="7.25" />
            <path d="m13 7-1.8 4.2L7 13l1.8-4.2Z" />
          </svg>
          Explore the themes
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="md:justify-self-end">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-sm md:max-w-none">
        <Lightbox
          src={frontispiece}
          widths={[480, 720, 1080]}
          sizes="(min-width: 768px) 24rem, 90vw"
          alt={alt}
          caption="Frontispiece of Leviathan, engraved by Abraham Bosse in consultation with Hobbes, 1651. British Library copy, via Wikimedia Commons."
          class="w-full rounded border border-stone-200 shadow-[0_24px_48px_-24px_rgb(0_0_0/0.45)] dark:border-stone-800"
        />
      </div>
      <figcaption class="mt-3 text-center text-xs text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">
        Engraved by Abraham Bosse, 1651. Click to enlarge.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto mt-20 max-w-2xl">
    <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-stone-500 uppercase dark:text-stone-400">About the frontispiece</h2>
    <div class="prose-chapter mt-4 font-serif text-lg leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        The engraving is the argument of the book in a single image. A giant wearing a crown looks out over a
        peaceful landscape: fields, a river, a walled town with its church, a few figures going about their
        business. His body is made of people, hundreds of them, standing close together and facing inward, so
        that the sovereign is nothing but the subjects who compose him. This is the common-wealth as Hobbes
        defines it in <a href="/chapters/17-of-the-causes-generation-and-definition-of-a-common-wealth/" class="chapter-link">Chapter 17</a>: one person, of whose acts a great multitude have made themselves the author.
      </p>
      <p>
        In his right hand he holds a sword, the civil power; in his left, a bishop's crozier, the ecclesiastical
        power. Hobbes's point, argued at length in Parts III and IV, is that both belong to the same hand. The
        two columns of panels beneath repeat the pairing. On the sword's side: a castle, a crown, a cannon, a
        trophy of weapons, and a battle. On the crozier's side: a church, a mitre, a thunderbolt of
        excommunication, the forked distinctions of scholastic logic, and an ecclesiastical court. Each is the
        civil or spiritual counterpart of the other.
      </p>
      <p>
        Across the top runs a line from the Book of Job, chapter 41, where God describes the sea monster
        Leviathan: <em>Non est potestas Super Terram quae Comparetur ei</em>, "There is no power on earth which
        can be compared to him." Hobbes took his title from the same verses. The creature that nothing on earth
        can match is the state, and the picture shows what it is made of.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-6 text-xs text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">
      British Library copy of the 1651 edition, via Wikimedia Commons. Public domain.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
